CorpFam is released under two licences, because it contains two different kinds of thing.

Underlying rights, stated first because it explains the choice below. The source records
are United States federal contract award and entity registration data. 17 U.S.C. 105(a):
"Copyright protection under this title is not available for any work of the United States
Government." SEC EDGAR filings are likewise public-domain government work. There is
therefore no upstream copyright in the source records for us to license out, and no
reason to impose downstream restrictions on data derived from them.

One non-copyright constraint applies, and is documented rather than ignored. SAM.gov's
Terms of Use grant only a limited licence over "D&B Open Data", which includes legal
business name, and forbid disseminating it in bulk for records tied to base awards dated
before 4 April 2022. The definitive test named in those terms is an Entity Validation
Service source field that the award data does not carry, so the exposure is bounded rather
than measured. Both a full and a conservative post-cutoff release tier ship for that
reason. See the "Provenance and redistribution" section of the paper and
experiments/results/dnb_provenance.json.


